WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.907
Integer sizes and allocation math are part of memory safety.

2
00:00:03.907 --> 00:00:08.399
Buffers are finite, and the values that describe them are finite too.

3
00:00:08.399 --> 00:00:16.863
When code calculates a size, an offset, a count, or a capacity, that arithmetic must match the object that will be accessed later.

4
00:00:16.863 --> 00:00:20.314
Allocation math must distinguish bytes from elements.

5
00:00:20.314 --> 00:00:26.369
An element count multiplied by the size of each element produces a byte count for allocation.

6
00:00:26.369 --> 00:00:31.969
A byte count is not the same as an index count, a string length, or a structure count.

7
00:00:31.969 --> 00:00:35.680
Naming and helper functions should make the unit visible.

8
00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.870
Multiplication for allocation sizes can overflow.

9
00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:45.381
Addition can also overflow when code adds a header, terminator, alignment padding, or trailing data.

10
00:00:45.381 --> 00:00:51.371
If the arithmetic wraps or truncates, the allocation may be smaller than later code expects.

11
00:00:51.371 --> 00:00:56.124
Check the arithmetic before calling allocation functions or copying data.

12
00:00:56.124 --> 00:01:01.919
Signed and unsigned comparisons can produce surprising results when values are converted.

13
00:01:01.919 --> 00:01:07.713
A negative index or length should be rejected before it is converted to an unsigned size.

14
00:01:07.713 --> 00:01:11.815
A wrapped size should not be trusted as a large valid capacity.

15
00:01:11.815 --> 00:01:16.243
Defensive code validates both the type range and the business range.

16
00:01:16.243 --> 00:01:20.930
Structure layout, padding, and alignment should not be guessed casually.

17
00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:25.879
The compiler controls layout according to the platform, ABI, and type rules.

18
00:01:25.879 --> 00:01:39.096
Code that serializes, copies, or overlays structures should be careful about what bytes are meaningful, what padding may exist, and whether the external representation truly matches the in-memory object.

19
00:01:39.096 --> 00:01:43.458
Casts should not be used to hide type, size, or ownership problems.

20
00:01:43.458 --> 00:01:53.485
A cast can request a conversion, but it does not prove the value fits, the pointer is aligned, the lifetime is valid, or the destination owns the storage.

21
00:01:53.485 --> 00:01:59.801
When a cast appears near memory access, reviewers should ask what contract makes the access safe.
